ction g of x  as a geometric series where the common  ratio is x  instead of the r that were more typical  more typically used to represent the  common ratio so n equals 0 to infinity  of a times x to the n so what's the  difference between these two things well  this is just a special case we're making  c equals 0 and we're making all of these  a sub 0 a sub 1 a sub 2 they're all just  going to be equal to a  and we know that we can expand this out  as a times x to the 0  plus a  plus a  a times x to the first power plus a  times x squared and we just go on and on  and on forever this is an infinite this  is an infinite series  well we've already seen how we can take  this sum in fact we've already done  multiple examples where we take sums of  infinite series and we've derived the  formula that gets us that this is going  to be equal to a over 1 minus our common  ratio and in this case our common ratio  is equal  is equal to x  what's interesting about this is instead  of going from the sum to kind of or  instead of going from this geometric  series expanded form to what the sum  needs to be we can use this to go the  other way if we can get some functions  into this form we can then expand them  out as geometric series or essentially  we will find their power series  representation because a geometric  series is a special case of a power  series  and to show an example of that  let's take  let's take the function  h of x  h of x is equal to  1 over 3 plus x squared so i encourage  you now to pause this video and try to  rewrite this function in this form right  over here and if you can write it in  this form to then go and write it as a  geometric series  so i'm assuming you've given a go at it  so let's try to do it so we want a 1  right over here and we want to subtract  something so if we want a 1 right over  here let's divide the denominator by 3.  so this is equal to or let's factor out  a 3 i should say 1 over 3  times 1  plus  x squared  x squared  over three all i did here is i factored  out a three i divided three by three  factor it out and then i i factored out  a three from x squared so if i were to  redistribute you would get this again  now i could rewrite this  i could rewrite this as instead of 1  over 3 i could write this as let me  write it this way i could write it as  i could write it as so this part right  over here  i could write as 1 3  over  over  1  and i want to subtract this thing  instead so 1 minus  1 minus and i could write  negative  x squared  negative x squared over 3. notice adding  x squared over 3 is the same thing as  subtracting negative x squared over 3.  so now i've written in this form i have  my a my a would be 1 3 and then my  common ratio x is going to be negative x  squared over 3. so now i can rewrite h  of x i can say that h of x  h of x  is going to be equal to  the infinite series so go from n equals  zero to infinity my a is one third  my a is one third and then my common  ratio  my common ratio  is  negative x squared  negative x squared over three  i'm going to raise that to the nth power  and so if i wanted to expand it out and  actually maybe i'll write that in the  purple if i wanted to expand this out  what would it look like well this would  be this would be equal to well the first  term when n is equal to zero this whole  thing just becomes one so this is going  to become this is just going to be  one-third  and then the next term i'm just going to  multiply by the common ratio i'm going  to multiply by negative x squared over  3. so this is going to be equal to this  is going to be equal to negative  negative so if i take 1 3 and multiply  by negative x squared over 3 that's  going to be negative  x squared over  9.  and then if i were to multiply by  negative x squared over 3 again well now  i'm going to get positive  x  x to the 4th  over  over 27. notice the common ratio every  time to go from here to there i have to  multiply by negative x squared over 3.  to go from there there i have to  multiply by negative x squared over 3  and i could keep going on  and on and on and if i really wanted to  write it in that form i could just let  me let me make this a little bit clearer  so i could just write it as i could just  write this as 1 3  1 3  minus  1 9  times x  times x squared  plus  1 27  times x to the fourth and you might say  well this is almost the same form but  you know all these forms up here we had  right here you just have the x to the  zero term and then the x squared term  what about this well remember you could  just make this coefficient zero so i  could even i could even rewrite it like  this i could say this is equal to one  third  one third times  x to the zero  plus  0 times x to the first power  plus or let me write this minus  1 9  x squared  and then plus  0 times x to the third power  and then plus 1 over 27 times x to the  fourth power and then notice i have this  in the exact same form i have the exact  same form as our original power series  so without using any calculus we were  able to take a function like this  represent it  represent it like this and then find a  power series representation for it and i  would this one i don't want to confuse  you here but you could ignore these  terms because these are just zero and  one of these two is kind of a clean way  of viewing it

